    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that analogy actually helps a lot. so if the insulin are the keys, i guess the problem is like there aren't enough keys or the locks are broken? \n\ncan you explain how things like high stress or even just lack of sleep can mess with that \"gatekeeper\" process? i've heard that can cause my numbers to jump even if i didn't eat much.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly, you've got the right idea—it's either not enough keys, or the locks are becoming resistant to the keys. When you're stressed or sleep-deprived, your body releases stress hormones, like cortisol. These hormones are like a sudden, frantic command from the central command, telling the body to hoard energy, which makes the cells temporarily ignore the insulin keys. So, even if you haven't eaten much, those hormones cause your liver to dump extra sugar into the bloodstream, effectively jamming the gates!",
      "source": "target",
